  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Precision + Fast Turning Calibration\n\nImprove the current flight controls while preserving the rolled-back baseline.\n\n## Core Problem\n\nThe current mouse steering has a precision-versus-turning conflict:\n\n- High sensitivity makes small mouse movements produce excessive rotation.\n- Lower sensitivity improves precision but makes large directional changes too slow.\n\nA single linear sensitivity multiplier cannot adequately solve this problem.\n\n## Required Approach: Nonlinear Mouse Response Curve\n\nImplement a configurable nonlinear response curve.\n\nDesired behavior:\n\n1. Small mouse movements produce subtle, predictable rotation for precise aiming.\n2. Medium movements progressively increase the steering response.\n3. Large movements produce substantially stronger steering for fast directional changes.\n4. The response must remain continuous and predictable.\n5. No sudden sensitivity spikes or abrupt transitions.\n\nUse a mathematically defined response curve with a clearly documented input normalization range.\n\n## Independent Parameters\n\nKeep these settings separate:\n\n- Base mouse sensitivity.\n- Response curve exponent or equivalent curve parameters.\n- Maximum yaw rate.\n- Maximum pitch rate.\n- Yaw/pitch sensitivity balance.\n- Optional minimal input smoothing.\n\nDo not use one multiplier to control all these behaviors.\n\n## Important Flight Behavior\n\n- Preserve direct arcade mouse steering.\n- Preserve working Pointer Lock and `movementX` / `movementY`.\n- Do not introduce virtual joystick behavior.\n- Avoid excessive smoothing and angular inertia.\n- When mouse input stops, do not continue accumulating unwanted rotation.\n- Keep yaw and pitch independently configurable.\n- Keep the existing aim boundary independent of the response curve.\n\n## Calibration\n\nBefore choosing final values, inspect the existing implementation and establish the current input range and turn-rate calculations.\n\nUse conservative initial parameters. Do not invent commercial-game reference values.\n\nTest at least:\n\n- Very small mouse movements.\n- Slow continuous tracking.\n- Medium mouse movements.\n- Large directional changes.\n- Rapid direction reversals.\n- Mouse input stopping immediately after a turn.\n\nMake only the changes required for this calibration.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Small corrections are controllable.\n- Target tracking feels predictable.\n- Large mouse movements can turn the ship quickly.\n- No abrupt acceleration spikes.\n- No unwanted drift.\n- Pointer Lock remains continuous at physical screen edges.\n- No regressions to combat, camera architecture, or other game systems.\n\nReport the exact parameters changed and the results of browser testing. Preserve the previous baseline so this calibration can be reverted independently."
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Precision + Fast Turning Calibration\n\nImprove the current flight controls while preserving the rolled-back baseline.\n\n## Core Problem\n\nThe current mouse steering has a precision-versus-turning conflict:\n\n- High sensitivity makes small mouse movements produce excessive rotation.\n- Lower sensitivity improves precision but makes large directional changes too slow.\n\nA single linear sensitivity multiplier cannot adequately solve this problem.\n\n## Required Approach: Nonlinear Mouse Response Curve\n\nImplement a configurable nonlinear response curve.\n\nDesired behavior:\n\n1. Small mouse movements produce subtle, predictable rotation for precise aiming.\n2. Medium movements progressively increase the steering response.\n3. Large movements produce substantially stronger steering for fast directional changes.\n4. The response must remain continuous and predictable.\n5. No sudden sensitivity spikes or abrupt transitions.\n\nUse a mathematically defined response curve with a clearly documented input normalization range.\n\n## Independent Parameters\n\nKeep these settings separate:\n\n- Base mouse sensitivity.\n- Response curve exponent or equivalent curve parameters.\n- Maximum yaw rate.\n- Maximum pitch rate.\n- Yaw/pitch sensitivity balance.\n- Optional minimal input smoothing.\n\nDo not use one multiplier to control all these behaviors.\n\n## Important Flight Behavior\n\n- Preserve direct arcade mouse steering.\n- Preserve working Pointer Lock and `movementX` / `movementY`.\n- Do not introduce virtual joystick behavior.\n- Avoid excessive smoothing and angular inertia.\n- When mouse input stops, do not continue accumulating unwanted rotation.\n- Keep yaw and pitch independently configurable.\n- Keep the existing aim boundary independent of the response curve.\n\n## Calibration\n\nBefore choosing final values, inspect the existing implementation and establish the current input range and turn-rate calculations.\n\nUse conservative initial parameters. Do not invent commercial-game reference values.\n\nTest at least:\n\n- Very small mouse movements.\n- Slow continuous tracking.\n- Medium mouse movements.\n- Large directional changes.\n- Rapid direction reversals.\n- Mouse input stopping immediately after a turn.\n\nMake only the changes required for this calibration.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Small corrections are controllable.\n- Target tracking feels predictable.\n- Large mouse movements can turn the ship quickly.\n- No abrupt acceleration spikes.\n- No unwanted drift.\n- Pointer Lock remains continuous at physical screen edges.\n- No regressions to combat, camera architecture, or other game systems.\n\nReport the exact parameters changed and the results of browser testing. Preserve the previous baseline so this calibration can be reverted independently."
    }
  }
}
