  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Key Discrepancies**\n\nI\'ve finally pinpointed the core of the issue! It\'s all about the interplay between the codebase and the original prompt. I\'m deep-diving into the specifics, comparing the \"before\" state of `src/engine/InputManager.ts`, specifically how it handles Pointer Lock and relative `movementX`/`Y`. I\'m meticulously tracing the execution path.\n\n\n**Isolating Persistent Joystick Bug**\n\nI\'ve traced the issue to `src/engine/AimBoundary.ts` and `FlightController.ts`. The implementation of a persistent \"Virtual Joystick\" model is the culprit. `FlightController.ts` accumulates `mouseDeltaX` in `this.joystickX`, preventing the joystick from returning to zero when the mouse stops. This violates the prompt\'s requirement for \"direct arcade mouse steering.\" The aim boundary uses `computeRoundedRectBoundary` and `clampToRoundedRect`, but is indirectly broken by the joystick implementation.\n\n\n**Refining Steering Response**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a nonlinear response curve to solve the precision-versus-turning conflict. My focus is ensuring small mouse movements deliver subtle rotation, while medium movements progressively increase the steering response. Large movements should rapidly achieve full rotation. I\'m exploring different curve implementations. I\'m prioritizing independent configuration for yaw and pitch.\n\n\n**Defining Steering Parameters**\n\nOkay, I\'m now setting up independent parameters to fine-tune the steering response. I\'m focusing on a mathematically defined curve. The critical needs are predictable and continuous responses; I\'ll avoid sudden sensitivity changes. I\'m also implementing separate controls for base sensitivity, curve parameters, and maximum yaw/pitch rates to avoid a single multiplier. I have to preserve the previous baseline so calibration can be reverted.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "7zf_arX-NeHnqtsP6az-2As"
