  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Current Steering Config**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the virtual joystick parameters within the `steering.ts` config file, specifically examining `aimBoundaryWidth` and `aimBoundaryHeight`. These parameters, currently set to 0.3 and 0.35 respectively, are critical to the responsiveness of the steering interface. This is where I am focused right now.\n\n\n**Refining Control Parameters**\n\nI\'ve moved on from the initial joystick boundary dimensions to delve into other control parameters within the `steering.ts` configuration. I\'m especially interested in the interdependencies between `mouseSensitivity`, `precisionSensitivity`, and `responseCurve` and how they contribute to the overall steering feel. I\'m now looking at the core joystick implementation in `FlightController.ts`. Specifically, the persistent non-zero `joystickX` and `joystickY` values upon mouse stop, which is unexpected behavior.\n\n\n**Calibrating Nonlinear Response**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a nonlinear mouse response curve to address the precision-versus-turning conflict. My focus is on achieving subtle rotation with small movements, progressive steering with medium movements, and strong steering for large movements. The goal is a continuous, predictable response without any abrupt changes in sensitivity. I\'m investigating how to normalize the input range to ensure a well-defined curve.\n\n\n**Isolating Core Steering Parameters**\n\nI\'m now focusing on keeping base mouse sensitivity, response curves, max yaw/pitch rates, and sensitivity balance as independent parameters. I am intentionally preserving arcade mouse steering, Pointer Lock functionality, and direct `movementX`/`movementY` control. My goal is to avoid virtual joystick behavior and excessive smoothing. I also want to prevent unwanted rotation accumulation when mouse input stops, while keeping yaw and pitch independently configurable.\n\n\n**Clarifying Flight Characteristics**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the specific flight behaviors the calibration requires, emphasizing direct arcade mouse steering. The focus is on implementing small corrections, predictable target tracking, and rapid directional changes with larger mouse movements. Abrupt acceleration spikes, drift, and Pointer Lock issues at screen edges are definite no-gos. I\'m making sure that I do not regress any other game system and am planning on reporting the exact parameters that were changed. I am making sure the previous baseline is preserved. I want to emphasize, this is DIRECT mouse steering, not virtual joystick!\n\n\n**Eliminating Virtual Joystick Influence**\n\nI\'m now laser-focused on eliminating any residual virtual joystick influence and unwanted smoothing. I\'m taking steps to ensure rotation stops immediately when mouse input ceases. Specifically, I\'m testing mouse input stopping immediately after a turn and confirming there\'s no drift. I\'m ensuring the existing aim boundary remains independent of the mouse response curve. The key is to deliver direct arcade mouse steering with a nonlinear response.\n\n\n**Confirming Independence, Baseline**\n\nNow I\'m making sure that mouse input directly controls rotation, with no drift when it stops, while keeping the aim boundary independent. I\'m also looking at the impact on `FlightController.ts` and `AimBoundary.ts`. I want to preserve the previous baseline configuration, perhaps as a `BASELINE_STEERING_CONFIG` in `steering.ts` and `FlightController.ts`. This calibrated direct arcade nonlinear response will be the new active steering mode.\n\n\n**Deeply Analyzing Core Integration**\n\nNow I\'m diving into the deeper math and component interactions required for this. Specifically, I am analyzing `InputManager`, `CameraController`, `CombatManager`, `FlightHUD`, and `SettingsPanel` to ensure a mathematically robust, zero-drift, phenomenal flight feel. I am paying close attention to the dataflow of raw mouse input, and where cursor, aim, and bore-sight values are used. I am focused on the inter-component relationships to ensure that the flight steering is rock-solid.\n\n\n**Verifying Frame Rate Independence**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on frame-rate independence. I\'ve realized directly applying a nonlinear curve to raw `mouseDeltaX` without considering frame time `dt` creates frame-rate dependency. I\'m focusing on ensuring consistent mouse velocity and rotation across different frame rates, normalizing input based on `dt` to achieve a smooth and predictable response.\n\n\n**Calculating Frame-Rate Independence**\n\nI\'m now calculating to determine the proper way to achieve frame-rate independence. It\'s critical that the total rotation isn\'t significantly less at high frame rates, or more at low rates. I am now working through the math of a nonlinear curve applied to raw per-frame mouse deltas. I need to make sure that the per-second mouse speed is used for the calibration.\n\n\n**Calculating Frame-Rate Independence**\n\nI\'ve determined frame-rate independence is achieved by applying the nonlinear curve to the per-second mouse speed, ensuring consistent angular velocity regardless of frame rate. Zero mouse delta always results in zero output, and smooth mouse movements, at any rate, produce predictable, frame-independent rotation. Single-pixel ticks at high frame rates now provide expected responses. Adding a fast exponential filter on velocity is an option.\n\n\n**Verifying Smoothing Sensitivity**\n\nI\'m now carefully verifying minimal input smoothing configurations to ensure rotation stops completely when the mouse input ceases. Specifically, I\'m testing `inputSmoothing` values around 0, 0.08, and 0.15 to check for any residual angular inertia or unwanted rotation accumulation. I am making sure the decay time remains fast enough and the steering remains responsive. I\'m also confirming that single-pixel mouse movements at high frame rates behave smoothly.\n\n\n\n**Refining Response Implementation**\n\nI\'m now implementing a new nonlinear mouse response curve, making it mathematically clean. I\'m focusing on ensuring zero angular rate on zero mouse input, and a fast exponential decay for minimal smoothing, if needed. My tests are verifying the responsiveness of small and large movements, while making sure medium movements are handled precisely.\n\n\n**Investigating Flight Characteristics**\n\nI\'m now carefully investigating the ship\'s existing turn rates, specifically yaw, pitch, and roll speeds, defined within `ships.ts`. This is critical as they become the basis for adjusting the nonlinear response. The goal is a responsive but controlled flight, matching these existing capabilities, not exceeding them.\n\n\n**Calculating Nonlinear Response Curve**\n\nI am now focusing on the core math for a continuous nonlinear mouse response. I\'m taking the raw mouse deltas, converting them to pixels per second, and then normalizing them against an `inputNormalizationRange`. I\'m verifying the system behaves the same for micro-aiming movements and larger, faster movements. I\'m focused on the curve\'s characteristics, to produce a very smooth continuous transition.\n\n\n**Calculating Power Curve Response**\n\nI\'m now carefully calculating the response of a pure power curve, versus a precision-floor + power curve, to better handle very small inputs. I\'m focusing on the effect of a power curve, and I\'m using the interceptor\'s max yaw rate of 2.8 rad/s (160\302\260/s) and a `normMaxPxPerSec` of 1800 px/s (30 px/frame) as my base. I\'m making sure to verify very small movements still have acceptable response.\n\n\n**Calculating Blended Response**\n\nI\'m now calculating a blended linear-power curve to address the precision aiming problem with pure power curves. I am also working through the math of a blended power curve, specifically to establish the parameters for a smooth transition from a linear response at low input, to the nonlinear curve for precision and agility. I need to make sure the combined curve still offers an excellent response for both slow and rapid mouse movements.\n\n\n**Optimizing Input Curve**\n\nI\'m now deeply immersed in the nuances of the proposed blended linear-power curve, and I\'m very excited by what I am seeing. I\'ve found an excellent formula, `g(u) = a + (1 - a) * u^p`, where the small movement response is now a predictable, subtle, 100% linear precision response that doesn\'t vanish to zero. It\'s awesome and will work very well. I am focusing on tuning this power curve for excellent mouse control.\n\n\n**Evaluating Curve Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now focusing on what happens when mouse input exceeds the normalized range and applying a soft ceiling to the output, like `tanh`. I\'m comparing this to a hard clamp.  I\'m also looking into the angular degrees per pixel for various mouse movement speeds, to check against the design prompt and test cases. The focus is to make sure our math is rock solid.\n\n\n**Calculating Angular Maneuvers**\n\nI\'m now calculating precise angular turns for each of the test scenarios, focusing on Interceptor\'s yaw and pitch rates. I\'m carefully evaluating the impact of the blended linear-power curve on these maneuvers. I\'m determining the relationship between the mouse input, angular velocity, and the time required for specific directional changes. I\'m focusing on making sure the maneuvers feel right, at different mouse speeds.\n\n\n\n**Verifying Nonlinear Output**\n\nI\'m now carefully verifying the output calculations of the blended linear-power curve, with the parameters a = 0.28 and p = 1.85, and checking smooth clamping vs hard clamp, and C1 smoothness. I\'m focusing on the effect of each configuration on the overall angular rotation speed, with specific attention on the small movements. I\'m also now making sure the math lines up with the desired max turn rate.\n\n\n**Validating Response Curves**\n\nI\'m now deeply immersed in validating these results with an interceptor, using a blended linear-power curve. The test table results reveal specific angular changes per 100px mouse movement at various speeds. These results are incredibly encouraging, and I am focused on the interplay between the blended curve and actual in-game maneuvers.\n\n\n**Refining Nonlinear Curve**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the blended linear-power curve\'s sensitivity at slow mouse speeds and its potential impact on precision and larger maneuvers. I\'m focusing on the mathematical characteristics of the curve to create the desired flight feel. I\'m verifying that the response to very small inputs is sufficiently sensitive. I\'m looking into how to smooth the mouse movement and make sure everything is rock solid.\n\n\n**Validating Precision Flight**\n\nI\'m now carefully checking the responsiveness and realism of the Interceptor at high speeds.  I\'m focusing on validating these values:  micro-aiming at 5.7 degrees/100px, brisk turns at 7.6 degrees/100px, and rapid directional changes exceeding 10 degrees/100px. I\'m verifying maneuvers feel right.\n\n\n**Evaluating Steering Parameter Adjustments**\n\nI\'m now deep in the final evaluation, verifying angular changes with the refined blended linear-power curve. The test results confirm Interceptor maneuvers feel responsive. Precision aiming is at 3.7 degrees/100px. Brisk turns yield 4.2 degrees/100px, with rapid directional changes over 10. The calculations confirm the math is solid, offering a very smooth experience.\n\n\n**Confirming Nonlinear Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now confirming the blend linear-power curve\'s dynamics align with expected turn rates. Focusing on the calculated angular changes. Micro-aiming provides a 3.7\302\260/100px response, while large movements yield ~12\302\260/100px and 180\302\260/s at 25px/frame. The increase is now 3.25X on the blend! This blend provides precision and responsiveness.\n\n\n**Verifying Curve Smoothness**\n\nI\'m now implementing the smooth, C2-continuous soft-ceiling saturation function, ensuring no kinks even at max yaw/pitch. This is vital to the continuous, predictable response. The blend between the curve and soft saturation, using $\\tanh$, ensures C1, with the same value and first derivative (1.0) at `raw = 0.85` , and the curve has a second derivative of 0.0 at the join, for the smoothest possible transition.\n\n\n**Evaluating Radial Implementation**\n\nI\'m now carefully evaluating radial versus per-axis application of the blended linear-power curve to the 2D mouse velocity vector. I\'m considering independent yaw/pitch sensitivity and max rates, while examining the differences in the diagonal movement. I am considering these parameters in relation to the overall smoothness and responsiveness.\n\nThe previous tests confirmed no sharp corners or flat clipping jolts. The response curve function $s(x)$ with `m = 0.85` and `h = 0.15` and C2 saturation with `tanh` is working flawlessly. Specifically, at $x = 1.0$, $s(1.0) = 0.964$, and at $x = 1.25$, $s(1.25) = 0.9986$. The `p = 1.0` linear user setting, the formula `raw = a * u + (1 - a) * u^1 = u`, works as it should, strictly linear to 0.85, and smoothly C2-continuous approaching `1.0`.\n\n\n**Evaluating Radial Implementation**\n\nI\'m now carefully evaluating the radial application method, testing it against per-axis applications of the blended linear-power curve. I\'m focusing on preserving separate yaw and pitch sensitivities, independently clamping each axis. I am verifying that diagonal movements maintain the expected responsiveness and smoothness, while ensuring the math is solid and accurate.\n\n\n**Validating Sensitivity Calculation**\n\nI\'m now diving into the calculations for applying yaw and pitch sensitivities and max turn rates independently. I\'m focusing on validating that my blending methods and curve implementation function correctly for both horizontal, vertical and diagonal mouse movements, and that the independently-configurable `maxYawRate` and `maxPitchRate` values are respected!\n\n\n**Refining Isotropic Implementation**\n\nI\'m now refining the isotropic 360-degree smoothness implementation, making sure zero diagonal deadening exists. The blend formula ensures independent yaw/pitch sensitivity with no clipping. Multiplying by `yawSensitivity` and `pitchSensitivity` before C2 soft-saturation seems ideal. I\'m verifying the individual calculations of radial velocity and curve gain. Also, I am carefully reviewing the per-axis angular demands.\n\n\n**Calculating Hull Agility**\n\nI am now focusing on integrating the ship profile ratios, using existing yaw/pitch speeds, to ensure distinct handling feel. I\'m confirming `effectiveMaxYaw` and `effectiveMaxPitch` calculations, with `targetYawRate` and `targetPitchRate` updates. I\'m verifying that the parameters are correctly preserving the Interceptor\'s distinct handling characteristics.\n\n\n**Finalizing Optimal Steering**\n\nI\'ve finalized the nonlinear steering parameters: `precisionScale`, `maxYawRate`, `maxPitchRate`, and `yawSensitivity`/`pitchSensitivity`. The `inputSmoothing` value provides control over minimal exponential smoothing. The formula, blending a linear precision response with a power curve, maintains a C2 continuous behavior, guaranteeing an excellent feel for the player.\n\n\n**Verifying Optimal Stopping**\n\nI\'m now deep in the math, calculating stopping behavior. With the proposed parameters (`inputSmoothing = 0.08` & boost), angular velocity drops to nearly zero in the first frame. The second is essentially zero. I\'ve re-examined the old `FlightController` damping and acceleration values to confirm that these also snap to zero when the user stops moving the mouse, eliminating any drift.\n\n\n\n**Optimizing Drift Elimination**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the implications of the \"no drift\" requirement, scrutinizing `FlightController.ts`. My key finding is the original code had an acceleration-based stop, with 12 frames of drift after mouse stop!  This, with `inputSmoothing > 0`, adds up to a bad feel.  The new method, with `inputSmoothing = 0`, is now an instant stop, as requested! The user\'s specific demands are directly addressed.\n\n\n**Calculating Optimal Stopping**\n\nI\'ve determined a method for achieving instantaneous stopping with nearly zero angular drift. This involves using two separate smoothing constants. For non-zero input, the standard smoothing value, which filters sub-frame jitter. During mouse stop, a second smoothing value nearly snaps the angular velocity to zero on the first frame, and essentially zero on the second, as designed.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing Stopping Implementation**\n\nI\'m now implementing an instant-stop solution. I\'ve found even minimal residual angular velocity after mouse stop is unacceptable. I\'m focusing on directly setting angular velocity to zero when input ceases, with a tiny, imperceptible decay over the first two frames, and then zeroing if the change is sufficiently minor, to meet the requirements for instant stopping.\n\n\n\n**Evaluating Reticle Integration**\n\nMy current focus is on integrating the HUD\'s aim reticle, and ensuring correct behavior within the context of direct arcade mouse steering. I\'m scrutinizing how `aimCursorX/Y` and `aimScreenX/Y` will respond when the mouse input stops, ensuring they accurately reflect the immediate cessation of rotation. I want to keep the virtual reticle, the same as before, but with the new flight behavior.\n\n\n**Refining Reticle Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the aim reticle to respond correctly to direct arcade steering changes. I\'m ensuring it smoothly centers with zero mouse input, staying within the aim boundary during maneuvers. I\'m focusing on the reticle\'s deflection within the bounded area to provide crisp visual feedback, and verifying that weapon fire aligns with the ship\'s boresight.\n\n\n**Refining Reticle Behavior**\n\nI\'m now integrating the aim reticle and ensuring precise visual feedback with the new steering. I\'m confirming subtle reticle deflection inside the boundary during turns and clamping smoothly against it with rapid movements. Key is immediate, drift-free centering upon mouse stop, and that the boundary is completely independent of the response curve.\n\n\n**Validating Reticle Alignment**\n\nI\'m now implementing the reticle deflection driven by ship velocity, and everything seems accurate. The reticle smoothly centers on mouse stop, and smoothly touches the aim boundary with rotation. Precision adjustments barely shift the reticle, providing the necessary visual feedback. I\'m verifying weapon fire aligns consistently with the new boresight.\n\n\n**Validating Reticle Alignment**\n\nI\'m now integrating the reticle with `CombatManager.ts` and `CameraController.ts`, confirming that the existing lead pip and chase camera functionality remain unaffected. I\'ve ensured a smooth transition with no disruption to combat or the camera behavior. The aim reticle now provides the right level of precise visual feedback.\n\n\n**Preserving Baseline Parameters**\n\nI\'m now establishing a clean system for independent baseline preservation within `steering.ts`. I\'m creating `BASELINE_STEERING_CONFIG` for the old, unmodified settings and `DIRECT_MOUSE_STEERING_CONFIG` for the calibrated parameters. The active config, `STEERING_CONFIG`, now uses a `steeringMode` flag to switch between the two.\n\n\n**Finalizing Config Parameters**\n\nI\'ve now fully integrated the parameters within `steering.ts`, including `maxPitchRate`, `maxYawRate`, `yawSensitivity`, `pitchSensitivity`, and the optional `inputSmoothing`, while keeping aim-boundary parameters completely independent. Also, I\'ve created a dedicated `MouseResponseCurve.ts` module, which contains the mathematical formula and details. Everything is rock solid.\n\n\n**Implementing Steering Configuration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the finalized parameters and curve calculations into `FlightController.ts`. Specifically, I am focusing on ensuring that the `vx` and `vy` math works and the new `g(u)` response curve is correctly applied, ensuring smooth transitions and no dead zones. Also, C2-continuous soft saturation and max angular rates are verified.\n\n\n**Verifying Zero-Drift Implementation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the stopping behavior. The system provides immediate stops with imperceptible decay, confirmed by zero frame-heap allocations, as designed. The aim and clamp methods in `FlightController.ts` are also verified, with tests confirming the reticle is integrated properly.\n\n\n**Validating Complete Implementation**\n\nI\'m now confirming complete integration and ensuring all features are working as designed. I\'ve verified the aim boundary, independent of the response curve, plus instant-stop implementation. The virtual reticle accurately centers and weapon fire aligns with the boresight. The arrow key steering, roll, boost, thrust, and visual banking remain unaffected. The \'virtual-joystick-baseline\' is also preserved with a one-line toggle, and settings migration is ready.\n\n\n**Integrating Steering Settings**\n\nI\'m now integrating the finalized parameters into the `SettingsPanel.tsx` UI, specifically, `Mouse Sensitivity`, `Response Curve Exponent`, `Max Yaw Rate`, `Max Pitch Rate`, `Yaw Sensitivity`, `Pitch Sensitivity`, and `Input Smoothing`. These values are exposed as sliders. The UI design will match the existing design.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Feedback**\n\nI\'m now integrating reticle updates within `FlightHUD.tsx`, ensuring `deadzoneRectRef` is hidden in the new steering mode, keeping the HUD clean. I\'m focusing on ensuring `aimReticleRef` and `steerLineRef` smoothly reflect direct steering outputs without any regressions. I need to make sure the visual feedback is excellent.\n\n\n"
